---
/**
 * The section that must not be softened. It sits outside the `Section`
 * rhythm on purpose: a warning that looks like every other card reads as
 * marketing, and this one is the honest limit of the current release.
 */
---

<section id="security" class="defer-offscreen-rendering border-border-subtle border-t px-6 py-20">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <div
      class="animate-fade-in-up opacity-0-initial border-warning/35 bg-warning/[0.04] rounded-xl border p-7 sm:p-9"
    >
      <p class="text-label-md text-warning font-mono uppercase">What you are agreeing to</p>

      <h2 class="text-headline-md mt-3">
        An agent you connect can run anything inside that project
      </h2>

      <div class="text-body-lg text-foreground-muted mt-5 space-y-4">
        <p>
          <strong class="text-foreground">A new project allows everything</strong> until you narrow
          it. From the dashboard you can set it to read only, name the commands it may run, refuse
          specific ones in any mode, choose which tools exist, and ask to confirm every change
          before it happens. Paths stay confined to the project root either way, but that
          confinement is not a sandbox: a command that runs still runs as you, with your environment
          and your network.
        </p>
        <p>
          An allow list with <code class="text-foreground font-mono">shell</code> turned on is only
          a suggestion. Confirmation only reaches a person who is there: in the conversation on MCP
          2026-07-28, and otherwise on the machine's terminal or in the dashboard. Revoking a
          machine from the dashboard closes its connection immediately, so that is the stop button.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-body-md text-foreground-faint border-warning/20 mt-6 border-t pt-5">
        The full model is in{" "}
        <a
          href="/docs/policy/"
          class="text-foreground underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
        >
          what a project allows
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
